This is where this upper bound is being used:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/0a68c2029a1dcf962b6f488c860fa062b41c1262/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/src/System/Text/Json/Serialization/Metadata/ReflectionEmitMemberAccessor.cs#L79-L106

I might be missing something, but this restriction seems artificial to me. The constructor is compiled to a `Func<object[], T>` delegate so arities should be practically unlimited. Assuming the original concern was codegen size, we can always fall back to using a delegate that wraps `constructorInfo.Invoke`:
```C#
if (parameterCount > JsonConstants.MaxParameterCount) 
{
    return (args => (T)constructorInfo.Invoke(args)!);
}
```
Related to #33444. cc @ layomia

@krwq and @eiriktsarpalis 

I made this suggestion earlier to make it configurable and I am wondering what you think about it? 

> Perhaps making it configurable in [System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/main/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/src/System/Text/Json/Serialization/JsonSerializerOptions.cs) with a default if 64 might have been a way to help people out while you investigate the exact cause and reason of this hard-coded rule. They have done this exact same thing for backward compatibility with [DefaultMaxDepth](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/main/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/src/System/Text/Json/Serialization/JsonSerializerOptions.cs#:~:text=set%3B%20%7D%20%3D-,DefaultMaxDepth,-%3B) in JsonSerializerOptions. They have defaulted it to 64 as well.
